{"obs_mr3q4xy8_8f83ab24110f":{"observation":{"hookType":"reasoning","id":"obs_mr3q4xy8_8f83ab24110f","raw":{"messageID":"msg_f23adceda002VQ38EaS7sBxV6E","text":"Got it! The viewer accepts `Host: 127.0.0.1:3113` and `Host: localhost:3113` but rejects `192.168.1.10:3113` and `0.0.0.0:3113`.\n\nThe issue is: when you open the viewer in a browser at `http://192.168.1.10:3113`, the browser will send `Host: 192.168.1.10:3113` which gets rejected.\n\nI need to add `192.168.1.10` to the viewer's allowed hosts. But the viewer is part of the agentmemory Node.js process - how do I configure it?\n\nLet me check if there's an env var or config option for the viewer's allowed hosts. Looking at the Content-Security-Policy response header:\n```\nconnect-src 'self' http://localhost:* http://127.0.0.1:* ws://localhost:* ws://127.0.0.1:* wss://localhost:* wss://127.0.0.1:*\n```\n\nThis shows the CSP is hardcoded to allow only localhost and 127.0.0.1 connections. The \"forbidden host\" check is separate from CSP though.\n\nThe \"forbidden host\" response with 403 is coming from a host header validation. Since this is baked into the agentmemory Node.js code, I can't easily change it through config.\n\nI can use socat to rewrite the Host header. Or use a reverse proxy (nginx) to rewrite it and also modify the CSP headers.\n\nLet me configure socat to send with Host: 127.0.0.1:3113. But socat is a raw TCP forwarder - it can't modify HTTP headers directly.\n\nActually, I can use socat with an `OPENSSL` method or wrap with a script. Or better yet, I can use nginx as a reverse proxy which is more appropriate for HTTP-level manipulation.\n\nLet me use a simple approach: write a small proxy script that rewrites the Host header.\n\nActually, the simplest fix is to use socat with a `SYSTEM` shell command that uses `nc` to inject the right host header. But that's not clean.\n\nBetter approach: use a simple node/http-proxy or python proxy. Or even simpler - `awk` with socat piping.\n\nActually, the absolute simplest approach is to install nginx and set up a reverse proxy with `proxy_set_header Host 127.0.0.1:3113;`.\n\nLet me check if nginx is available, or install it.\n\nWait - actually, there's an even simpler approach. Since the viewer just checks the Host header against a whitelist, I could try adding `192.168.1.10` to some agentmemory config. But that's in the Node.js source code.\n\nLet me go with nginx. It's more robust for this use case.\n\nActually, let me try an even simpler approach first - just rewrite the Host header at the iptables level, which is not possible. Or use a simple TCP proxy that can modify headers.\n\nActually, the simplest approach: install nginx, set up a minimal reverse proxy.\n\nOr even simpler: use `proxy.py` or install a simple http proxy. Let me just go with nginx.\n\nActually wait - the user's browser sends the full URL in the Host header. The browser has `http://192.168.1.10:3113/` in the URL bar, so it sends `Host: 192.168.1.10:3113`. This is how all browsers work.\n\nTo make the viewer work in a browser, I need either:\n1. A reverse proxy that rewrites the Host header to `127.0.0.1:3113`\n2. OR modify the viewer code to accept external hosts\n\nOption 1 with nginx is the standard approach. Let me install nginx."},"sessionId":"ses_0de14025cffenyT5Dz8MGawUg4","timestamp":"2026-07-02T16:33:52.348Z"},"type":"raw"},"obs_mr3q547i_0708060aec18":{"observation":{"hookType":"post_tool_use","id":"obs_mr3q547i_0708060aec18","raw":{"attachments":[],"call_id":"call_00_P53Wg7VhIvoHfOzMRvMC5549","duration_ms":771,"metadata":{"description":"Install nginx on gate7","exit":0,"output":"debconf: unable to initialize frontend: Dialog\ndebconf: (Dialog frontend will not work on a dumb terminal, an emacs shell buffer, or without a controlling terminal.)\ndebconf: falling back to frontend: Readline\ndebconf: unable to initialize frontend: Readline\ndebconf: (This frontend requires a controlling tty.)\ndebconf: falling back to frontend: Teletype\